body_template:
Arrival is procedural. The colony authority sends a form.

The form asks: crew count, resource status, crop viability, hull integrity. The ship computer fills it in. The form does not ask how you feel.

{score_paragraph}

{crop_paragraph}

{crew_paragraph}

Colony Report #{report_number}, filed.

The destination appears on sensors. The ship computer announces arrival with the same tone it uses for everything.

{crew_reaction}

{score_paragraph}

The colony report is one sentence. It is the truest thing in the game.

Within a month of your arrival, the colony training office requests permission to use your voyage logs as instructional material.

They want the fuel curves. They want the crew rotation schedules. They want, specifically, the growbay maintenance intervals, which the reviewing agronomist has annotated with the single word 'correct' eleven times.

{crop_paragraph}

{crew_paragraph}

The request form has a box for compensation. You leave it blank. The training office interprets this as generosity. It was actually disbelief. You were making it up the whole way, same as everyone.

The course runs every spring. Nobody who takes it believes the logs are real.

Six weeks after arrival, you catch yourself complaining about the weather.

Not the radiation shielding. Not the recycler capacity. The weather — the ordinary, scheduled, dome-managed weather, which was drizzling when you wanted to walk to the depot.

This, the colony's oldest resident tells you, is the actual moment of arrival. Not the docking. Not the paperwork. The first time the new place is ordinary enough to complain about.

{crop_paragraph}

{crew_paragraph}

The ship sits in long-term berth, getting visited less each month. It doesn't mind. Ships don't mind things. But you visit anyway, now and then, and sit in the captain's chair, and complain to it about the weather.

The dock inspector walks the length of your ship twice, writing continuously.

The inspection form has a field labeled 'general condition.' The inspector has written, after visible deliberation: 'arrived.' Asked to elaborate, they gesture at the ship, which chooses that moment to shed a piece of exterior plating onto the dock. It rings like a bell. Nobody moves to pick it up.

{crop_paragraph}

{crew_paragraph}

The colony accepts you the way harbors accept weather-beaten ships: without ceremony, without judgment, and with an immediate work order. You made it. The word 'it' is doing some work in that sentence. So did you. So did everyone.

The plating stays where it fell for three days. Someone eventually plants flowers in it.

They name the landing valley after your ship. You find out from a road sign.

Nobody asks your permission for this, because that is not how naming works out here — a surveyor writes it on a chart because everyone was already calling it that, and then it is simply true, the way mountains are true.

{crop_paragraph}

{crew_paragraph}

Years on, a schoolchild will ask you what the crossing was like, and you will open your mouth to tell them about the fear, and instead you will hear yourself describing the way the stars looked from the bridge with the cabin lights off. Both answers are honest. You picked the truer one.

Eight months after you land, the second colony ship arrives, and you discover you have become the person who stands at the dock giving advice.

Don't trust berth seven. Water the north beds at dusk. The recycler tech takes bribes in coffee, not credits. You hear yourself say these things with the easy authority of somebody's grandmother, and you wonder when exactly the crossing stopped being the thing you were doing and became the thing you had done.

{crop_paragraph}

{crew_paragraph}

The new arrivals look at you the way you once looked at the void: like something vast that has to be crossed to get to the rest of their lives. You hand them a crate and tell them where the kitchen is. It is, you have decided, the whole job of having arrived.

The colony intake officer has a form for ships that arrive in your condition. You know this because she reaches for it before you finish docking.

Status: arrived. Condition: see attached. Attachments: several.

{crop_paragraph}

{crew_paragraph}

The ship goes into the repair yard and stays there so long the yard crew starts calling it by a nickname, which is how you learn that broken things, if they hang around long enough, get adopted. You visit on rest days with tools you barely know how to hold anymore. Some weeks you fix a panel. Some weeks you just sit in the hull and listen to it tick as it cools. Both count as repairs. The yard boss told you that, and the yard boss does not say kind things carelessly.

The colony gives you a hero's welcome, and you stand in it like rain you can't feel.

They are not wrong to cheer. Something did arrive. The ledger just reads differently from the inside: every cheer has a name it should have gone to instead.

{crop_paragraph}

{crew_paragraph}

A year on, the colony administrator asks if you'd captain a supply run. Short hop. Milk run. You say no, and then you say it again in your quarters that night, to nobody, to make sure it's still true. The stars outside the window are the same stars. That's the thing about the crossing nobody warns you about: it ends, and they don't.
